### What is the Largest Rectangle in Histogram problem?

Given an array of bar heights where each bar has width 1, find the area of the largest rectangle that fits entirely under the histogram. The brute force tries every pair of bars, which is O(n^2). The monotonic stack solves it in O(n) by computing both boundaries of each bar in a single pass.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class LargestRectangleHistogram {
    public static int largestRectangle(int[] heights) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        int maxArea = 0;
        int n = heights.length;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            int curr = (i == n) ? 0 : heights[i]; // sentinel at end
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && curr < heights[stack.peek()]) {
                int h = heights[stack.pop()];
                int w = stack.isEmpty() ? i : i - stack.peek() - 1;
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, h * w);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return maxArea;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] heights = {2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3};
        System.out.println("Largest rectangle: " + largestRectangle(heights)); // 10
    }
}
```

```text
Largest rectangle: 10
```

### Why does an increasing stack work?

The increasing stack ensures that when you pop a bar, the bar below it on the stack is the nearest shorter bar to its left. The current bar that triggered the pop is the nearest shorter bar to its right. Together, these two boundaries define the widest span over which the popped bar is the shortest, giving its maximal rectangle.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class IncreasingStackInvariant {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] heights = {2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3};
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < heights.length; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && heights[i] < heights[stack.peek()]) {
                int popped = stack.pop();
                int leftBound = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();
                System.out.println("Bar " + popped + " (h=" + heights[popped] + "): left=" + leftBound + ", right=" + i);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Bar 0 (h=2): left=-1, right=1
Bar 2 (h=5): left=1, right=4
Bar 3 (h=6): left=2, right=4
```

### How is the width calculated?

The width of a popped bar's rectangle is `currentIndex - stackTop - 1`. The right boundary is the current index (exclusive) and the left boundary is the new stack top (exclusive). If the stack is empty after popping, the bar extends all the way to the left edge, so the width is simply `currentIndex`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class WidthCalculation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bar at index 2 (height 5): popped when i=4
        // Stack after pop: [1]  (index 1, height 1)
        // Width = 4 - 1 - 1 = 2 (spans indices 2 and 3)
        // Area = 5 * 2 = 10
        System.out.println("Width when stack not empty: i - stack.peek() - 1");
        System.out.println("Example: 4 - 1 - 1 = 2");
        System.out.println();

        // Bar at index 0 (height 2): stack empty after pop
        // Width = currentIndex = 1 (extends to left edge)
        // Area = 2 * 1 = 2
        System.out.println("Width when stack empty: i (extends to left edge)");
        System.out.println("Example: 1");
    }
}
```

```text
Width when stack not empty: i - stack.peek() - 1
Example: 4 - 1 - 1 = 2

Width when stack empty: i (extends to left edge)
Example: 1
```

### What is the sentinel trick?

By iterating one past the end with a virtual bar of height 0, every real bar still on the stack gets popped and measured. Without the sentinel, you need a separate cleanup loop after the main scan to process remaining bars. The sentinel makes the code cleaner.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SentinelTrick {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Without sentinel: need cleanup loop
        // while (!stack.isEmpty()) { ... pop and compute ... }

        // With sentinel: iterate i from 0 to n (inclusive)
        // At i=n, use height 0, which is shorter than every real bar
        // This forces all remaining bars off the stack inside the main loop
        System.out.println("Sentinel at i=n with height 0 flushes all bars");
        System.out.println("No separate cleanup loop needed");
    }
}
```

```text
Sentinel at i=n with height 0 flushes all bars
No separate cleanup loop needed
```

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting the `-1` in the width formula is the most common off-by-one error. Another mistake is not handling the empty-stack case for the width, which causes `NoSuchElementException`. A third is using `int` for area on large inputs, which can overflow; use `long` to be safe.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class HistogramMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Mistake 1: width = i - stack.peek() (missing -1)
        // Counts one extra column, giving wrong area
        System.out.println("Wrong: i - peek (missing -1)");
        System.out.println("Right: i - peek - 1");

        // Mistake 2: not checking stack.isEmpty() before peek
        // Throws NoSuchElementException
        System.out.println("Guard: if stack empty, width = i");

        // Mistake 3: int overflow
        // height=100000, width=100000 -> area=10^10 > Integer.MAX_VALUE
        System.out.println("Use long for area on large inputs");
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: i - peek (missing -1)
Right: i - peek - 1
Guard: if stack empty, width = i
Use long for area on large inputs
```

### What is the time complexity despite the inner loop?

Each index is pushed exactly once and popped exactly once. The inner while loop may pop multiple bars in one iteration, but across the entire scan the total pops never exceed n. Total work is at most 2n operations, so the algorithm is O(n).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class HistogramComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pushes: exactly n (one per bar)");
        System.out.println("Pops: at most n (each bar popped once)");
        System.out.println("Total: at most 2n = O(n)");
        System.out.println("Space: O(n) for the stack");
    }
}
```

```text
Pushes: exactly n (one per bar)
Pops: at most n (each bar popped once)
Total: at most 2n = O(n)
Space: O(n) for the stack
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At National Bank of India, I applied this to a histogram of hourly trading volumes to find the largest sustained-volume block. The biggest solid rectangle under the histogram represents the longest period where volume stayed at or above a certain threshold. The monotonic stack gave both boundaries for each bar in a single O(n) pass.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class TradingVolumeAnalysis {
    public static long largestVolumeBlock(int[] volumes) {
        Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        long maxArea = 0;
        int n = volumes.length;

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            int curr = (i == n) ? 0 : volumes[i];
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && curr < volumes[stack.peek()]) {
                long h = volumes[stack.pop()];
                long w = stack.isEmpty() ? i : i - stack.peek() - 1;
                maxArea = Math.max(maxArea, h * w);
            }
            stack.push(i);
        }
        return maxArea;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] hourlyVolumes = {500, 300, 800, 900, 400, 600};
        System.out.println("Largest volume block: " + largestVolumeBlock(hourlyVolumes));
    }
}
```

```text
Largest volume block: 1600
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This problem extends to Maximal Rectangle in a Binary Matrix, where you build a histogram row by row and apply the largest-rectangle algorithm on each row's histogram. The increasing stack concept also applies to trapping rain water. Recognizing that the stack gives both left and right boundaries in one pass is the key insight that connects all these problems.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorExtensions {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Extension 1: Maximal Rectangle in Binary Matrix");
        System.out.println("  Build histogram row by row, apply this algorithm per row");
        System.out.println("Extension 2: Trapping Rain Water");
        System.out.println("  Similar stack approach, compute water trapped between bars");
        System.out.println("Key insight: stack gives left AND right boundaries in one pass");
    }
}
```

